// Opening range breakout. ORB_POS = (close - range_low) / (range_high -
// range_low) against the session's opening range: the high and low of the
// first 30 minutes after the host's session open. At or above 1 means the
// close broke above the range high. The host publishes ORB_POS only once the
// range window has closed - before that the range is still growing and the
// signal is absent, not zero. VOL_RATIO(20) is the existing volume / sma(volume,
// 20) convention.
// REGIME: a session with a decisive opening auction that then trends. Do NOT
// fire on a quiet tape where price drifts into the level - that is why the
// participation term exists.
// CONDITIONS: a 5m close at or above the range high, with volume at least 1.5x
// its twenty-bar average behind it. The closing bar is the hold; a wick through
// the high that closes back inside does not count.
// INVALIDATION: a close back inside the opening range. A failed opening break
// usually reverses through the other side of the range.
// SHAPE: 5m; fast enough to catch the break, and no faster than the host can
// publish a closed bar against a fixed range.
// NOT donchian_breakout: that breaks a rolling N-period channel that keeps
// moving with every bar. This range is anchored to a session boundary the host
// defines, stops growing when the range window closes, and is the same level
// for every participant that day.
// CALIBRATED ON: US equity index ETF, regular session, 30-minute opening range.
// ORB_POS is scale-free so the threshold travels; the session itself does not.
// The host owns where the session starts, the window length, holidays and half
// days, and what "the session" means for a 24-hour instrument. Nano has no
// clock and no session concept, so without that boundary this rule is
// meaningless.
strategy OpeningRangeBreakout {

    every 5m {

        if ORB_POS >= 1 and VOL_RATIO(20) >= 1.5 {

            buy(SPY, 0.65)

        }

    }

}
